  "query_preview": "..\n(Monday night through Saturday)\nIssued at 140 AM EDT Sun Oct 11 2026\n\nAs the boundary slides southward across the region Monday night \ninto early Tuesday, a dry continental airmass will make a long-\nawaited return to South Florida. While this will not be a \"cold\" \nor even \"cool\" front by any metric (consider keeping the fall gear\nin the wardrobe for now), the change in the air will certainly be\nnoticeable as we drop from forecasted dewpoints in mid to upper \n70s on Monday to dewpoints in the upper 60s to low 70s on Tuesday.\nEven more remarkable will be the drop in precipitable water \nvalues, deterministic and ensemble guidance suggest a potential of\nseeing precipitable water values across South Florida below the \n10% percentile for this time of year. No significant temperature \nchange is expected behind this front during the daytime hours \n(forecast highs in the upper 80s to low 90s Tuesday and Wednesday)\nbut a slight drop in overnight temperatures is forecast with the \npotential of low temperatures in the mid to upper 60s across \ninland southwestern Florida on Tuesday morning. \n\nThe advertised reprieve from moisture will be a short lived one as \nthe frontolytic boundary and envelope of associated moisture lift \nback across the region late Wednesday into Thursday as an expansive \narea of surface high pressure becomes established across the \nsoutheastern United States. Mid-level ridging briefly established \nacross the southeastern United States during the early to mid \nportion of the work week will quickly flatten by Thursday as the \nnext lobe of troughing slides eastward across the northern United \nStates. As the positively tilted trough amplifies and digs across the \neastern United States, a strengthening surface low will strengthen \nand push eastward in tandem. While this is at the tail end of the \nextended period, ensemble and deterministic model guidance depict the \narrival of a frontal boundary in the vicinity of South Florida late \nin the work week into the upcoming weekend. Deep tropical moisture \nin the warm sector out ahead of the boundary could result in higher \nrain chances towards the end of the work week with drier and cooler \nweather behind it. For now however, it's still way too early to \nspeculate on exact forecast details.",
